{"bill":{"id":"119-s-345","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"345","title":"SHUSH Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-30","latestActionDate":"2025-01-30","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Finance.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Consumer Product Safety Commission","Consumer affairs","Criminal procedure and sentencing","Federal preemption","Firearms and explosives","Law enforcement officers","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000577","cosponsorBioguideIds":["S001217","R000618","C001114","M001198","C001056","C001075","G000359","G000608"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Silencers Help Us Save Hearing Act or the SHUSH Act This bill removes silencers from regulation under federal firearms laws. Specifically, it removes silencers from the list of firearms subject to regulation (i.e., registration and licensing requirements) under the National Firearms Act (NFA). Additionally, it excludes a muffler or silencer from the list of firearms subject to regulation (e.g., background check requirements) under the Gun Control Act of 1968 (GCA). Additionally, the bill does the following: preempts state or local laws that tax or regulate firearm silencers, specifies that a person who lawfully acquires or possesses a silencer under provisions of the GCA meets the registration and licensing requirements of the NFA, eliminates mandatory minimum prison terms for a crime of violence or drug trafficking offense in which a defendant uses or carries a firearm equipped with a silencer or muffler, and permits active and retired law enforcement officers to carry a concealed silencer. Finally, the bill excludes firearm mufflers and silencers from regulation by the Consumer Product Safety Commission. (Current law generally excludes firearms from regulation by the commission.)","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/345","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s345.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:02.610Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-345","plainSummary":"This bill would remove firearm silencers (also called suppressors) from the federal registration, licensing, and background-check requirements that currently apply to them under the National Firearms Act and Gun Control Act. It would also block state and local governments from taxing or regulating silencers, eliminate mandatory minimum prison sentences for crimes involving silencers, allow active and retired law enforcement to carry concealed silencers, and prevent the Consumer Product Safety Commission from regulating them.","industries":[{"industry":"firearms","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly deregulates silencers, reducing compliance costs and expanding the legal market for manufacturers and dealers of these products."},{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Retailers selling firearms accessories would face changed regulatory obligations and potentially expanded market access for silencer sales."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"e6eece5846606b0979ed9e69","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:55:15.007Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would remove silencers from regulation under federal firearms laws.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys things. People who own or buy guns.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what you can buy, and what it costs. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-s-345","bioguideId":"M001198","cycle":2026,"score":0.010434695351526318,"matches":[{"industry":"retail-consumer","dono